/*!
    \page opcua-server.html
    \title OPC UA Server
    \previouspage OPC UA Client
    \nextpage Working with a Server

    Server Studio lets you design an OPC UA server, run it locally, secure it, and
    simulate values — without writing code. The designed server runs as a \e separate
    process (the headless \b OpcUaServerRuntime, which embeds open62541), controlled by
    the GUI through \l ServerStudio and \l ServerRuntimeController (see \l {Architecture}).

    \section1 Server projects

    A server project (\c .uaserver) is a JSON file holding everything the runtime needs:
    the custom namespaces, the address-space nodes, any custom enumeration data types,
    the endpoint configuration, the security settings, and the behavior rules (\l{ServerProject}{ServerProject::ProjectData}).
    Projects are created, opened, saved, and closed from Server Studio.

    \section1 Designing the address space

    In the studio you build a tree of nodes (\l ServerNodeModel):

    \list
        \li \b Objects to organize the address space;
        \li \b Variables with a data type and an initial value;
        \li custom \b{enumeration} data types, referenced by variables;
        \li custom \b namespaces that own your nodes.
    \endlist

    An address space can also be brought in rather than drawn by hand:

    \list
        \li \b{Import/export NodeSet2} — exchange the model as standard OPC UA
            NodeSet2 XML (\l{ServerProject}{ServerProject::NodeSet}).
        \li \b{Clone from a browsed server} — capture the address space of a server you
            are connected to in the client into a new server project.
    \endlist

    The project is checked by a validator before it is built or started
    (\l{ServerProject}{ServerProject::Validator}).

    \section1 Endpoint and security

    The endpoint configuration sets the port and application name. The security
    configuration selects the security policy and the accepted identities:

    \list
        \li \b{No security} for quick local testing;
        \li \b{Basic256Sha256} with signing and encryption, using a self-signed server
            certificate created in the server's own PKI store;
        \li \b anonymous access and/or \b{username/password} users.
    \endlist

    These settings let you exercise a client against realistic security without an
    external server.

    \section1 Running the server

    Starting the server launches the runtime process; the studio waits for it to report
    its endpoint and then shows it as running. Live diagnostics — session count, secure
    channels, and uptime — stream back to the studio over a local side-channel
    (\l DiagnosticsServer). You can stop, restart, or forcibly kill the runtime; the
    controller reports crashes and lets you reconnect.

    \section1 Simulating values

    Variables can be given a simulation so their values change over time while the server
    runs — counter, toggle, random, sine, or ramp (\l SimulationEngine). The runtime
    writes the simulated values into the server, so connected clients see real
    data-change notifications, which is useful for testing trends and subscriptions.

    \section1 Reacting to writes (behavior rules)

    A variable can be given \e{behavior rules}: when a client writes the variable, the
    runtime performs one or more actions on other variables. An action either writes a
    fixed value or copies the value that was just written, optionally after a delay. This
    reproduces the request/response handshakes an HMI expects from a PLC — for example,
    writing \c PageRequest makes the server set \c PageResponse — which a static
    address-space snapshot cannot. Rules are stored in the project and evaluated by the
    runtime through OPC UA write callbacks (\l RuleEngine).
*/
